welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Steven Roach a senior fellow and  senior lecturer with the Jackson  Institute for Global Affairs and Yale  School of Management mr. Roach has spent  28 years in senior positions at Morgan  Stanley the bulk of that time as chief  economist and more recently as chairman  of the firm's Asian businesses in  addition to his position at Yale he  remains the non-executive chairman of  Morgan Stanley Asia mr. Roach has long  been one of Wall Street's most  influential economists his most recent  book the next Asia opportunities and  challenges for a new globalization  analyzes Asia's economic imbalances and  the dangers of the region's dependence  on Western consumers today we'll talk  with mr. Roche about the future of China  and what it means for the global economy  welcome mr. Roche good to see you  Maryland in the past 30 years or so  China has experienced unprecedented  economic development let's talk about  that for a little bit and if you can  give us an overview of what has happened  well you go back 30 years ago Maryland  China Post Cultural Revolution the  economy was on the brink of collapse and  then a series of reforms were instituted  under the leadership of done shopping  and the economy is recorded on average  about 10% growth a year for three  decades predominantly led by two key  sectors exports and export led fixed  investment and the growth rate has truly  been spectacular the increases in the  standard of living the income for the  average Chinese has moved up tenfold  over that period no country has ever  experienced a development miracle like  that  the question that I raised and the  question that I address in my new course  at Yale the next China is is is this  model sustainable and Mike  conclusion which we can talk about in  greater detail is that there needs to be  some big changes in China's approach to  economic growth and development okay and  what are those changes  well the main change is that China has  to figure out the policies that needs to  put in place to stimulate internal  private consumption the model as it is  been evident over the past 30 years is  hugely dependent on not just China's own  competitiveness in the global economy  but on the state of global demand and in  the aftermath of this devastating  financial crisis that's the big question  mark the clouds that horizon  consumers in America Europe Japan are  going to be much less dynamic and  supporting the external demand that  underpins the Chinese export machine so  lacking in that support from the broader  global economy China needs a new source  of growth and that growth has got to  come from its 1.3 billion people in the  form of a significant increase in  internal private consumption okay you  recently wrote an op-ed piece for The  New York Times and you know you cite  some challenges for the US government  and then also there are some tensions  some some similar tensions that are  similar between the two countries let's  talk about a little bit about that well  China's the whipping boy for the  post-crisis world especially in in the  US where we have a horrific unemployment  and underemployment problem that  involves between 16 to 17 percent of the  entire American working age population  and for those Americans who are still  working their wages adjusted for  inflation have really not grown much for  the median American worker in the last  decade and unfortunately you know we  have a pretty poisonous political  climate in this country and and our  politicians in Washington don't want to  accept any responsibility for  or these problems bearing down on  American middle-class workers so they  blame globalization they blame the trade  deficit they blame the biggest piece of  our trade deficits with China they  conclude that China manipulates its  currency and therefore we should put  enormous pressure on China to revalue  the remin be sharply higher if they  don't let's slap them with trade  sanctions this is the view in Washington  the US House of Representatives enacted  a bill that would impose trade sanctions  on China if it doesn't follow this  recipe and yet unfortunately I believe  this whole analysis this prescription is  dead wrong  it reflects America's unwillingness to  look in the mirror and figure out what  really causes the savings problem and  what we must do as a nation to address  that okay so what do you think the  United States should do and what do you  think China should be doing in terms of  working together to try and resolve  these issues well from the US point of  view it's not that difficult to figure  out that we don't have just a china  problem if you look at our trade  statistics last year 2009 we had trade  deficits with 90 countries that's nine  zero that means excluding China we had  deficits with 89 other countries we have  a multilateral trade imbalance not a  bilateral trade problem simple math  simple economics tells you it's pretty  logical that you don't fix a  multilateral problem with a so-called  bilateral fix the reason we have a  multilateral trade deficit in the United  States is for one simple reason we do  not save lacking and saving we must  import surplus savings from abroad in  order to grow and run huge current  account and multilateral trade  imbalances to attract the capital if we  want to fix our trade problems with  China or with the other 89 countries we  were on deficits with we've got to  figure out how to save again and that  means we've got to control our budget  deficit eventually and encourage  American families to save the  old-fashioned way which they gave up  doing during the era of  excess when we saved out of asset  bubbles so these are our problems these  are not China's problems in the same  sense China must be responsible for its  surplus saving which has certainly  played a role in creating major global  imbalances and it must put in place  policies to stimulate an internal  private consumption I wrote about that  in detail in the New York time it's very  specific recommendations as to what I  think China needs to do and what what  are those recommendations well you have  to start with the idea that China's  consumption share of its GDP is only  about 36% right now and amazingly enough  that's about half the the ratio that  exists in the US so you know we consume  too much they consume too little there  are three things that need to be done  explicitly by the Chinese to boost  internal private consumption first and  foremost they have to invest in a social  safety net this is social security  private pensions unemployment insurance  medical insurance they do that they will  draw down the excesses of fear driven  precautionary saving number two they  have to provide a lot of support to  rural income still 750 million Chinese  live at relatively impoverished levels  in the rural countryside so aggressive  tax policy rural land reform IT enabled  connectivity and policies that encourage  rural urban migration need to be  emphasized the third and finally believe  it or not China despite all its growth  doesn't generate enough jobs and that's  because most of the growth is driven by  a manufacturing sector where you boost  productivity by substituting machines  for people China has a very small and  undeveloped services sector which is a  much more labor intensive endeavor of  economic activity and it needs to  promote policies that would encourage  the expansion of industries like  wholesale trade retail trade healthcare  hospitality leisure domestic  transportation you put those policies in  place and I'm very optimistic that China  will do that in its upcoming 12th  five-year plan to be enacted early next  year and the consumption share will move  the idea you can achieve that by a  currency revaluation on the part of  China  I think is ludicrous and far-fetched at  best okay do you think that currency  revaluation will happen though  nevertheless the Chinese have been since  the middle of 2005 been allowing their  currency the renminbi or some people  refer to it as you want to move up  gradually visa vie the dollar it's up  since the middle of 2005 a five year  period about 23% against the dollar  by the way when American congressman  first started bashing China back in  nineteen in 2005 they said that the  Chinese renminbi was 25% undervalued  against the dollar the dollar is up 23  of those 25 points they are still saying  that the remedy is 25% undervalued I  think their models are stuck they need  to sort of reboot the Machine and figure  out a new way to calculate these  so-called valuation metrics okay in  looking at China again is there are  there any specifics that China can do to  help struggling United States there's a  huge one if China succeeds in  implementing the policies that I just  enumerated and they boost their internal  private consumption as a share of u.s.  GDP that is an enormous opportunity for  American companies that export into  China is America's third largest export  market today the Obama administration  desperate for new sources of economic  growth has identified exports as a main  target for economic growth they feel  strongly that exports have the potential  to double over the next five years and  this would create millions of new jobs I  agree with that I think that's a very  ambitious but realistic target but how  are we going to double export markets if  we slap trade sanctions on the economy  that is our third largest export market  most likely they would  reciprocal sanctions on American goods  sold there if we encourage China to  boost internal private consumption then  we've got a shot to compete into that  market our third largest export market  and grow our way back to prosperity  rather than try to bash China as a  recipe for prosperity there's no way  we're going to achieve those goals if we  impose trade sanctions on China but if  we encourage China and put pressure on  them to open up their markets and grow  their internal demand that's an  extraordinary competitive opportunity  for the United States ok and let's look  at the next two Asia  five ten years down the road what does  it look like to me the next Asia will be  a more balanced Asia one that derives  much greater support from Asia's most  precious asset it's arena half billion  consumers the Asian a growth machine for  most of the last 3040 years has been  export led hugely dependent on demand in  the developed world that demand is now  tired it needs a rest that's what the  crisis is telling you and so it's it's  time for Asia given the amazing success  that the region has accomplished  especially China in figuring out and how  to crack the code of economic  development it's time for the region to  really put in place the types of  policies that I have elaborated on it  would stimulate its own internal markets  Asia will then be a more balanced  economy it will be a an economy that  derives much greater support from its  services sector less reliant on natural  resources as a result less of a polluter  in the world and this will be important  for a  village that is focused on climate  change and cleaner world GDP growth very  good thank you so much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work it's a pleasure to be with you  mural for more information about  professor Roach and his work please  visit our website at yale.edu backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at Yale
 

